      "For any recurring subject, define:\n- Species or object type.\n- Approximate size and proportions.\n- Distinctive markings or features.\n- Fur, material, or surface condition.\n- Accessories, if any.\n- Starting appearance.\nUse an image you have permission to use. Do not assume that an image found through a search engine is free to reuse. For real animals or people, avoid misleading viewers into believing a fictional AI-generated procedure is documentary footage.\nStep 3 — Generate scene images using references\nFor each scene:\n1. Describe the exact action.\n2. Include the established environment reference.\n3. Include the subject reference.\n4. Specify framing and camera angle.\n5. Describe the lighting and relevant props.\n6. State what must remain consistent.\n7. Generate and inspect the image before animating it.\nThe image prompt should describe one clear moment. Avoid packing several unrelated actions into a single image.\nStep 4 — Animate selected still images\nOnce a still image is acceptable, use the image-to-video function to create movement. The source's first animation attempt was considered too basic. The creator revised the opening visual by turning it into a Polaroid labelled “before,” then generated a new image showing gloved hands holding it. That revised composition produced a more engaging opening shot and also created a visual element that could return at the end.",
      "The lesson is to iterate on important scenes. Do not accept the first generation merely because it is usable.\nStep 5 — Inspect every generated clip\nCheck for:\n- Changes in the subject's identity or markings.\n- Distorted limbs, tools, or anatomy.\n- Objects appearing or disappearing.\n- Unnatural contact between hands, tools, and the subject.\n- Inconsistent room layout.\n- Unintended text or visual artefacts.\n- Motion that does not match the narration.\n- A camera movement that distracts from the action.\nThe source itself noted that AI-generated reference content can contain visual mistakes, such as implausible details. Treat those errors as quality-control issues, not as proof that every viewer will overlook them.\nContinuity checklist\nElement\tKeep consistent\nSubject\tIdentity, colour, markings, proportions\nEnvironment\tRoom layout, background, major props\nLighting\tDirection, intensity, time-of-day impression\nCamera\tPerspective and visual style\nTools\tAppearance and plausible use\nTransformation\tGradual progress from the established starting state",
