      "The figures are reproduced as reported in the source and may reflect rounded or slightly different dashboard snapshots. The source also mentions 30 subscribers during an earlier slower period, before the later jump. Do not treat every checkpoint as a perfectly continuous data series.\nWhat the experiment demonstrates\n1. A researched format can guide creative decisions.\n   The creator did not simply imitate the reference subject; they extracted a six-phase structure.\n2. A broadly understandable premise can lower the barrier to viewing.\n   The pet transformation could be understood quickly without specialist knowledge.\n3. The opening promise and final reveal can create a strong narrative loop.\n   The viewer is given a clear reason to continue.\n4. AI production still required human direction.\n   The creator selected the niche, analysed references, planned scenes, revised scripts, selected generations, improved the opening, edited the video, and evaluated results.\n5. Early distribution can be uneven.\n   A pause in views did not prevent later growth in this particular case.\n6. One successful upload is not a business model.\n   The experiment did not establish stable monthly income, long-term audience retention, repeatable virality, or monetisation approval.\nWhat it does not prove\n- That any new channel can reach hundreds of thousands of views in 24 hours.\n- That account age or watch history guarantees distribution.\n- That a particular retention percentage guarantees a push.\n- That waiting 12 hours after a flatline is the optimal upload rule.\n- That the reported views automatically translate into revenue.\n- That all AI-generated content is eligible for monetisation.\n- That one successful Short proves the niche has long-term demand."
