      "The source describes YouTube's “inauthentic content” policy as targeting content with minimal variation across uploads or content that is easily replicable at scale. It argues that the experiment involved substantial manual creative work.",
      "Treat that as the source's interpretation, not a guarantee of monetisation eligibility. Platform policies and enforcement can change. Eligibility depends on the actual channel and content, not merely on whether a human spent time prompting an AI tool.\nA more defensible production standard\nBuild videos where human creative decisions are visible in the result:\n- Original concepts rather than simple copies.\n- Distinct scripts and story progression.\n- Deliberate scene selection and editing.\n- Meaningful variation between uploads.\n- Consistent quality control.\n- A clear creative point of view.\n- Accurate representation of what is real and what is generated.\nDo not rely on the label “AI-enabled” as a policy exemption. It is a useful description of a workflow in which AI supports a human creative vision, but it does not override YouTube's rules.\nPractical policy checks before publishing\n- Review the current YouTube Partner Program requirements.\n- Review the current policy on inauthentic or repetitive content.\n- Check whether realistic altered or synthetic content requires disclosure under YouTube's current rules.\n- Avoid presenting fictional AI scenes as genuine documentary evidence.\n- Use music, images, voices, and other assets you have the rights to use.\n- Do not impersonate real people or mislead viewers about endorsements or events.\n- Reassess policy requirements as the channel evolves.\nThe source's claim that its content would be monetisable was an expectation, not evidence that the channel had already been accepted into the YouTube Partner Program or had earned revenue."
