      "The original experiment used a compressed schedule. The exact time allocation below is a practical adaptation of its sequence, not a claim that every creator can complete the same work in these exact windows.\nTime block\tMain task\tRequired output\nHour 1\tNiche research and format analysis\tThree analysed reference videos and a selected concept\nHour 2\tChannel setup and first-video ideation\tChannel identity, six-phase outline, draft script\nHours 3–5\tGenerate environment, subject, and scene assets\tApproved stills and animated clips\nHours 5–6\tVoice-over and editing\tComplete first Short\nHour 7\tQuality check and upload\tPublished Short with title and description\nHours 8–12\tMonitor initial performance and prepare next idea\tAnalytics log and second-video brief\nHours 12–16\tProduce a second video if resources allow\tSecond Short ready for review\nHours 16–24\tEvaluate the first upload and decide next action\tPerformance notes and next-upload decision",
      "The source's own production and reporting timeline extended beyond 24 hours. Its second video was completed after approximately two hours of work, but it was not uploaded while the first video was still receiving meaningful distribution. The final growth result was reported at hour 48.\nMinimum viable version\nIf you cannot complete the full workflow in one day, preserve the order of importance:\n1. Select a strong, original premise.\n2. Build a clear six-phase story.\n3. Generate a consistent set of visuals.\n4. Edit a complete, coherent Short.\n5. Publish with accurate metadata.\n6. Record analytics and learn from the result.\nDo not sacrifice the quality of the opening, visual continuity, or final reveal just to meet an arbitrary deadline."
